cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or data, please cite it as below."
title: "Agentic RAG Router: question-only routers and answer-level cascades for cost-aware multi-agent RAG"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-11-15
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24339546"
type: software
abstract: "A system with several retrieval-augmented agents of different cost can save compute by sending each question to the cheapest agent that will answer it correctly. We measure how much of that saving is reachable in practice. Five agents built from Qwen2.5 1.5B and 7B (no retrieval, one retrieval step, or iterative retrieval) answered 2,000 questions from NQ-Open, SQuAD, HotpotQA and MuSiQue over one pooled corpus, with every answer scored and its compute measured. On the 300 test questions an oracle that knows the cheapest correct agent reaches 61.0% accuracy at 61% of the compute of the strongest agent, which alone reaches 45.7%. We compare this ceiling with the fixed-agent mix, the best accuracy reachable at each compute by sending a fixed share of questions to each agent. A gated neural router, logistic regression and k-nearest neighbours over question embeddings all stayed within 1.5 points of that mix (5 seeds for the neural router; exact McNemar p ≥ 0.33 against the strongest agent), and the neural router was not better than the two simple baselines. Answer-level cascades, which run the small agents first and escalate when their answers disagree, refuse or look unreliable, did no better: the best matched the strongest agent's accuracy (46.0%) at 110% of its compute, because 92% of questions were escalated. Even a cascade that knew exactly when the small retrieval agent was right would reach only 50.0%. The remaining 11 points come from questions that only the other agents answered, so the oracle's gain reflects agents that are right on different questions, which neither the question nor the cheap answers revealed here. Data, code, all answers and costs are public."
